;; spec:   §7 (types §2.10, §6.4 closures, §8.4, §8.6)
;; expect: accept
;; result: 2
;; audit:  clean
;; swap! called directly with a closure taken from a struct field (a
;; heap closure: it was stored, types §6.5). swap!'s f is a :borrow
;; position (types §2.10: "f's colour unconstrained: f runs on the
;; calling thread; f is :borrow"), so the caller passes it with no count;
;; but f is then called through a function value, and
;;   types §8.4: "The closure convention (Decided, D5): env and every
;;   object argument are owned by the callee. The caller consumes the
;;   closure value and each argument ... L.code releases env — its own
;;   closure object — ... at every exit".
;; So whoever calls f inside swap! must hand it a count (retain it)
;; before the call. Result 1 + 1 = 2, clean.
;;
;; Observed (fibref run):
;;   failed:
;;   008-swap-with-heap-closure-double-release.fib:42:5: audit error:
;;   ReleaseOfFreed { id: ObjId(1) }
;; The closure's body releases env at its exit (explain: "before the
;; jump: release [env of <closure>]") without a matching retain by
;; swap!, so the closure's count drops by one per swap!; the struct's
;; drop then frees it and a later release is a release of freed memory.
;; Same with a let-bound closure that is also stored in a cell:
;;   (let ((a (atom 1)) (g (fn (x) (+ x 1))) (c (cell g))) (swap! a g))
;; and with a closure passed to an owned parameter:
;;   (defun keep (a g) (let ((c (cell g))) (swap! a g)))
;; Only stack closures (count operations are no-ops) and named functions
;; (immortal) pass, which is every swap! in cases/ownership.
;; Diagnosis: eval (crates/fibref/src/eval/cells.rs, Interp::swap invokes
;; f without retaining it). The spec's own pseudo-code for swap! (types
;; §8.6: "new = f(old)") also omits the retain of f that §8.4 requires of
;; any caller of a function value, so a compiler following §8.6 literally
;; has the same double free. Finding 005 (swap! as a function value) is
;; probably the same missing retain.
(defstruct H (f: (fn (i64) i64)))

(defun main () -> i64
  (let ((a (atom 1))
        (h (H (fn (x) (+ x 1)))))
    (swap! a (. h f))))
